      "text": "To solve this problem, let's denote the measure of the angle by \\( x \\) degrees. The complement of the angle is then \\( 90^\\circ - x \\) degrees. According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement. This relationship can be expressed with the following equation:\n\n\\[ x = 2(90^\\circ - x) \\]\n\nNow, let's solve this equation step by step.\n\n1. Distribute the 2 on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ x = 2 \\cdot 90^\\circ - 2 \\cdot x \\]\n\\[ x = 180^\\circ - 2x \\]\n\n2. To isolate \\( x \\), add \\( 2x \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ x + 2x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 3x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nSo, the measure of the angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\). Let's verify this by checking if it satisfies the original condition. The complement of \\( 60^\\circ \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ \\). Twice the measure of the complement is \\( 2 \\times 30^\\circ = 60^\\circ \\), which matches the measure of the angle.\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of the angle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
