      "sample_index": 6,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand what it means for an angle to be double its complement. Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **Define the Variables:**\n   - Let \\( x \\) be the measure of the angle in degrees.\n\n2. **Understand the Complement:**\n   - The complement of an angle is the angle that, when added to the given angle, equals 90 degrees. Therefore, the complement of \\( x \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\).\n\n3. **Set Up the Equation:**\n   - According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double its complement. This can be written as:\n     \\[\n     x = 2 \\times (90^\\circ - x)\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solve the Equation:**\n   - Distribute the 2 on the right-hand side:\n     \\[\n     x = 180^\\circ - 2x\n     \\]\n   - Add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to isolate \\( x \\) on one side:\n     \\[\n     x + 2x = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3x = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - Divide both sides by 3:\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = 60^\\circ\n     \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - The degree measure of the angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60}\n\\]",
